From 904c0400f093c4d59110497de634d3e0cdfce5bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:40:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix merging of identical functions whose address is taken

Linker::CombineSameConst folds linker objects with identical bytes and
references into one, and that includes function code. Two functions
that compile to the same code, e.g. empty stubs or dead stores, were
merged even when the program takes their addresses, so pointers to
different functions compared equal and function pointer tables held
the same entry twice. This happened at every level that enables
COPT_OPTIMIZE_BASIC (-O1 and up, -n and -bc); -O0 was unaffected.

Mark the linker object of a function with DTF_FUNC_VARIABLE (its
address is used as a value) with LOBJF_FUNC_VARIABLE and exclude such
objects from CombineSameConst. Functions that are only called directly
are merged as before.

Two related ways for distinct functions to share an address are closed
as well: a function whose address is only used through a cast to an
integer, (unsigned)f, was never given DTF_FUNC_VARIABLE because
GlobalAnalyzer::Analyze did not register the operand of EX_TYPECAST
(this also made its calling convention differ from an indirect call
through that integer), and Linker::Forwards appended a function that
is a bare JMP to its target, so both ended up at the same address.

Prepared with AI assistance (Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J5rsu7nnbQYnRY7GP34gdn
